// Decorative header watermark: a handful of countries near South Asia
// (public-domain, CC BY-SA 3.0, "Simple World Map" by Al MacDonald / Fritz
// Lekschas) — Afghanistan, Pakistan, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar,
// Sri Lanka, China, the Maldives, and a bit of Southeast/Central Asia —
// zoomed and cropped so India sits large and clear in front, with those
// neighbors visible (thin, subdued) around it for context.
//
// India itself is swapped for a separate, far more detailed outline (also
// public domain, Wikimedia Commons "India outline.svg"), pre-simplified
// (~475 points) and pre-transformed into this component's coordinate space
// — no <G transform> is used anywhere in this file. The old low-poly
// world map's own India shape is masked out (using a slightly dilated copy
// of the detailed outline, so the seam doesn't peek through) so only one
// clean line shows at India's real border.
//
// Two things that were NOT obvious and cost real debugging time:
// 1. preserveAspectRatio must be "none". This header is very wide and
//    short (~3.5:1); the usual "xMidYMid slice" cover-crops based on
//    whichever axis needs the *least* scale-up, which for this aspect
//    mismatch zoomed in so hard that India's own coordinates fell outside
//    the visible crop entirely. "none" stretches to fill both axes exactly
//    instead of cropping (a small, imperceptible-for-a-watermark aspect
//    distortion) and was the actual fix — not the earlier PNG asset
//    attempt, and not <G transform> (which was never the real culprit).
// 2. The viewBox must be tightly cropped to the content's real bounding
//    box (roughly x:496-698, y:361-528 in the source map's coordinate
//    space). An earlier, looser viewBox left empty bands above/below the
//    actual country shapes, so — even after fixing preserveAspectRatio —
//    the map only filled the middle of the header's height instead of
//    reaching edge to edge top and bottom.
const VIEW_BOX = '404 423 273 100';

